  "id": "voltage",
  "neon": "#5cd6ff",
  "note": "Rebuilt 2026-09-20 as a destination rather than a frontage. The old model was three dark boxes\nwith a marquee and a roof gantry — correct, and the thing you walked past on the way to somewhere\nelse. What it did not have was a silhouette, a surface, or a reason to be visible from anywhere\nbut the pavement outside it.\n\nFour decisions carry it.\n\nA DRUM ON A GLASS HALL. Nothing else in Coldwater at this size is round, so the profile is the\nbuilding's own from across the basin, and a mass that is not a box needs no signage to be\nrecognised. The hall under it is `ty_volt_frost` — etched glass, lit from behind — so the ground\nfloor is a lantern rather than a wall with holes in it, which is what a club actually looks like\nfrom the street at eleven at night. The stair core up the front-left corner is the same glass and\nis what gives the drum a vertical to stand against.\n\n⚠ CHROME IS A NEW MATERIAL FAMILY AND NOT A PALETTE SWAP. `ty_hf_chrome` and its neighbours are in\nPLAIN_WALL, whose own comment is exact about what they are: a seamless clad panel, flat fill, \"the\ndifference between chrome and pale stone\". Pale stone's opposite is not a mirror — `metal` there\nis 0.00 and there is no environment term at all — so a building faced in it reads as pale render.\nSee CHROME_WALL and matChrome in windshield.js, and the ⚠ on why PLAIN_WALL was not simply retuned.\n\nTHREE SIGNS AT THREE DISTANCES, AND NO TWO OF THEM SAY THE SAME THING. The gantry is for the air\nand the far side of the water and carries the NAME; the blade down the sign pylon is for the\nlength of the street and says 夜總會, the trade; the marquee is for the four metres between the\nrope and the door and says who is on tonight. That is the rule `SIGN_WORD`'s own header states —\nwhat makes a busy frontage read is not how many signs it carries but that they say DIFFERENT\nTHINGS, and three boards repeating one word look worse than one.\n\n⚠ THE BLADE TAKES `$trade` RATHER THAN THE LITERAL, which is the same argument one level down.\n`SIGN_HANZI` has said what a nightclub is since the derived trade blade shipped; copying that one\nword into this file would be a second copy to keep in step. The HAND picks the language — a\n`hanzi` font gets the Chinese word, anything else the English one.\n\n⚠ AND THE FINS ARE ON THE FLANKS AND THE BACK, NEVER THE FRONT. A pilaster rank stands PROUD of\nthe face it is on, so a rank up the entrance elevation crosses the marquee — a wire drawn over a\nname, which is exactly what `sign:fit` exists to catch. The flanks and the back are also where\n`models:elevation` says this city is bare, and a lit fin is the cheapest thing that fixes it.\n\n⚠ THE SEARCHLIGHTS ARE THE ONE PART THAT LEAVES THE BUILDING. See skyBeam in windshield.js: it is\na STROKE, because a beam is a shaft with a length in the world and a width on screen and that is\nwhat the stroke layer already expands; it is night-only, so it draws nothing at noon and that is\nnot a fault; and its duty is 1, the only cycle in the motion layer that never parks, because a\nsearchlight that stopped for twenty seconds would read as broken rather than as idle.\nRENDER_TUNE.motion 0 pins both of them at their home bearing — a beam standing still, never a\nbeam deleted.\n\nRENDER_TUNE.legacyArms puts the nightclub arm back.",
  "pal": "ty_volt_chrome",
  "replaces": "nightclub",
